    {
      "id": "d24BUIzWGCTrXCKM5xCYG",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 5636.937429346145,
      "y": 262.130057335515,
      "width": 2776.561767578125,
      "height": 1505,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#f08c00",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b1t",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 455569268,
      "version": 3,
      "versionNonce": 577728204,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1783254465463,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "## Variable Environment (VarEnv)\n\nAb humne **Lexical Environment** ke baare me padha, jo mainly `let` aur `const` bindings ko handle karta hai.\n\n**Variable Environment (VarEnv)** mainly **`var` declarations** aur **function declarations** ko handle karta hai.\n\n### Working\n\n* Variable Environment ka structure bhi Lexical Environment jaisa hi hota hai.\n* Iske paas bhi:\n\n  * Environment Record\n  * Outer Environment Reference\n* Variable lookup ka process bhi same hota hai (Scope Chain follow hoti hai).\n\n### Difference\n\n* **Lexical Environment** → `let`, `const`, class declarations.\n* **Variable Environment** → `var` aur function declarations.\n\n### Important Point\n\n`var` ki wajah se **Temporal Dead Zone (TDZ)** nahi hoti.\n\nAgar `var` variable declaration se pehle access karo, to JavaScript **ReferenceError** nahi deta, balki `undefined` return karta hai, kyunki hoisting ke time uski initial value `undefined` set ho jaati hai.\n\n```javascript\nconsole.log(a); // undefined\nvar a = 10;\n```\n\nLekin agar variable kisi bhi environment (current ya outer) me exist hi nahi karta, to result `undefined` nahi, balki **ReferenceError** hota hai.\n\n```javascript\nconsole.log(x); // ReferenceError: x is not defined\n```\n\n### Summary\n\n* Variable Environment aur Lexical Environment ki overall working kaafi similar hai.\n* Dono Environment Record aur Outer Environment Reference use karte hain.\n* Main difference sirf is baat ka hai ki kaunsi type ki declarations ko manage karte hain aur hoisting ka behavior alag hota hai.\n",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "## Variable Environment (VarEnv)\n\nAb humne **Lexical Environment** ke baare me padha, jo mainly `let` aur `const` bindings ko handle karta hai.\n\n**Variable Environment (VarEnv)** mainly **`var` declarations** aur **function declarations** ko handle karta hai.\n\n### Working\n\n* Variable Environment ka structure bhi Lexical Environment jaisa hi hota hai.\n* Iske paas bhi:\n\n  * Environment Record\n  * Outer Environment Reference\n* Variable lookup ka process bhi same hota hai (Scope Chain follow hoti hai).\n\n### Difference\n\n* **Lexical Environment** → `let`, `const`, class declarations.\n* **Variable Environment** → `var` aur function declarations.\n\n### Important Point\n\n`var` ki wajah se **Temporal Dead Zone (TDZ)** nahi hoti.\n\nAgar `var` variable declaration se pehle access karo, to JavaScript **ReferenceError** nahi deta, balki `undefined` return karta hai, kyunki hoisting ke time uski initial value `undefined` set ho jaati hai.\n\n```javascript\nconsole.log(a); // undefined\nvar a = 10;\n```\n\nLekin agar variable kisi bhi environment (current ya outer) me exist hi nahi karta, to result `undefined` nahi, balki **ReferenceError** hota hai.\n\n```javascript\nconsole.log(x); // ReferenceError: x is not defined\n```\n\n### Summary\n\n* Variable Environment aur Lexical Environment ki overall working kaafi similar hai.\n* Dono Environment Record aur Outer Environment Reference use karte hain.\n* Main difference sirf is baat ka hai ki kaunsi type ki declarations ko manage karte hain aur hoisting ka behavior alag hota hai.\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
