      "seed": 959675119,
      "version": 132,
      "versionNonce": 954780353,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1784584047398,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Promise chaainng \n\n# Promise Chaining\n\nPromise Chaining is a technique in which **multiple `.then()` methods are connected together** to execute asynchronous operations **one after another** in a sequence.\n\nThe main purpose of Promise Chaining is to **eliminate Callback Hell** and make asynchronous code more readable, maintainable, and easier to understand.\n\nOne of the biggest advantages of Promise Chaining is **Centralized Error Handling**. Instead of handling errors at every step, the entire Promise chain can be managed using a single `.catch()`.\n\nIf any Promise in the chain is **rejected** or an error is **thrown** inside any `.then()`, the Promise chain immediately changes to the **Rejected** state. JavaScript skips all the remaining `.then()` handlers\nand directly transfers control to the nearest `.catch()`, where the error is handled.\n\nThis mechanism is called **Error Propagation**, and it makes Promise Chaining much cleaner and more reliable than Callback Hell.\n\n### Flow\n\n```text\nPromise\n   │\n.then()\n   │     \n.then()\n   │\nError Occurs\n   │\n   ▼\nRemaining .then() are skipped\n   │\n   ▼\n.catch()\n   │\n.finally()\n```\n\n### Interview One-Liner\n\n> **Promise Chaining is a technique of connecting multiple `.then()` methods to execute asynchronous operations sequentially. It provides centralized error handling, where any error in the chain automatically\npropagates to the nearest `.catch()`, skipping the remaining `.then()` handlers.**\n ",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Promise chaainng \n\n# Promise Chaining\n\nPromise Chaining is a technique in which **multiple `.then()` methods are connected together** to execute asynchronous operations **one after another** in a sequence.\n\nThe main purpose of Promise Chaining is to **eliminate Callback Hell** and make asynchronous code more readable, maintainable, and easier to understand.\n\nOne of the biggest advantages of Promise Chaining is **Centralized Error Handling**. Instead of handling errors at every step, the entire Promise chain can be managed using a single `.catch()`.\n\nIf any Promise in the chain is **rejected** or an error is **thrown** inside any `.then()`, the Promise chain immediately changes to the **Rejected** state. JavaScript skips all the remaining `.then()` handlers and directly transfers control to the nearest `.catch()`, where the error is handled.\n\nThis mechanism is called **Error Propagation**, and it makes Promise Chaining much cleaner and more reliable than Callback Hell.\n\n### Flow\n\n```text\nPromise\n   │\n.then()\n   │     \n.then()\n   │\nError Occurs\n   │\n   ▼\nRemaining .then() are skipped\n   │\n   ▼\n.catch()\n   │\n.finally()\n```\n\n### Interview One-Liner\n\n> **Promise Chaining is a technique of connecting multiple `.then()` methods to execute asynchronous operations sequentially. It provides centralized error handling, where any error in the chain automatically propagates to the nearest `.catch()`, skipping the remaining `.then()` handlers.**\n ",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
