    {
      "id": "X_2y3_HIjlNba3kJcyc71",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 25516.898368860406,
      "y": 8871.95162808336,
      "width": 3255.109375,
      "height": 2870,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#e64980",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bAh",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 829008033,
      "version": 12,
      "versionNonce": 1326993679,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1784611525132,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "async Return a Value\nRule\n\nasync function hamesha Promise return karta hai.\n\nAgar aap normal value return karte ho, JavaScript usse automatically Promise.resolve(value) bana deti hai.\n\nExample 1: Normal Function\nfunction greet() {\n    return \"Hello\";\n}\n\nconsole.log(greet());\n\nOutput:\nHello\n\n\n\n\nExample 2: Async Function\nasync function greet() {\n    return \"Hello\";\n}\n\nconsole.log(greet());\n\nOutput:\n\nPromise { \"Hello\" }\nInternally JavaScript\n\nYe:\n\nasync function greet() {\n    return \"Hello\";\n}\n\nInternally almost aise behave karta hai:\n\nfunction greet() {\n    return Promise.resolve(\"Hello\");\n}\nValue Kaise Milegi?\n\nUsing await:\n\nasync function greet() {\n    return \"Hello\";\n}\n\nasync function main() {\n    const result = await greet();\n    console.log(result);\n}\n\nmain();\n\nOutput:\n\nHello\nPromise Style\nasync function greet() {\n    return \"Hello\";\n}\n\ngreet().then((result) => {\n    console.log(result);\n});\n\nOutput:\n\nHello\nTable\nCode        Return\nfunction test() { return 10; }        10\nasync function test() { return 10; }        Promise { 10 }\nawait test()        10\ntest().then(...)        10\nInterview Answer\n\nasync function hamesha Promise return karta hai. Agar hum normal value (return 10) return karte hain, to JavaScript usse automatically Promise.resolve(10) me wrap kar deti hai. Isliye value lene ke liye await ya .then() use karna padta hai.",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "async Return a Value\nRule\n\nasync function hamesha Promise return karta hai.\n\nAgar aap normal value return karte ho, JavaScript usse automatically Promise.resolve(value) bana deti hai.\n\nExample 1: Normal Function\nfunction greet() {\n    return \"Hello\";\n}\n\nconsole.log(greet());\n\nOutput:\nHello\n\n\n\n\nExample 2: Async Function\nasync function greet() {\n    return \"Hello\";\n}\n\nconsole.log(greet());\n\nOutput:\n\nPromise { \"Hello\" }\nInternally JavaScript\n\nYe:\n\nasync function greet() {\n    return \"Hello\";\n}\n\nInternally almost aise behave karta hai:\n\nfunction greet() {\n    return Promise.resolve(\"Hello\");\n}\nValue Kaise Milegi?\n\nUsing await:\n\nasync function greet() {\n    return \"Hello\";\n}\n\nasync function main() {\n    const result = await greet();\n    console.log(result);\n}\n\nmain();\n\nOutput:\n\nHello\nPromise Style\nasync function greet() {\n    return \"Hello\";\n}\n\ngreet().then((result) => {\n    console.log(result);\n});\n\nOutput:\n\nHello\nTable\nCode        Return\nfunction test() { return 10; }        10\nasync function test() { return 10; }        Promise { 10 }\nawait test()        10\ntest().then(...)        10\nInterview Answer\n\nasync function hamesha Promise return karta hai. Agar hum normal value (return 10) return karte hain, to JavaScript usse automatically Promise.resolve(10) me wrap kar deti hai. Isliye value lene ke liye await ya .then() use karna padta hai.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
