      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "🟢 Object.keys()\nTheory\n\nObject.keys() object ki saari own keys (property names) ko array ke form me return karta hai.\n\nExample\nconst user = {\n  name: \"Amar\",\n  age: 24\n};\n\nconsole.log(Object.keys(user));\nOutput\n[\"name\", \"age\"]\n\nInterview One-liner:\n\nObject.keys() object ki saari own keys ko array ke form me return karta hai.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n🟢 Object.values()\nTheory\n\nObject.values() object ki saari own values ko array ke form me return karta hai.\n\nExample\nconst user = {\n  name: \"Amar\",\n  age: 24\n};\n\nconsole.log(Object.values(user));\nOutput\n[\"Amar\", 24]\n\nInterview One-liner:\n\nObject.values() object ki saari own values ko array ke form me return karta hai.\n\n\n\n\n\n \n🟢 Object.entries()\nTheory\n\nObject.entries() object ki har property ko [key, value] pair ke form me array ke andar return karta hai.\n\nExample\nconst user = {\n  name: \"Amar\",\n  age: 24\n};\n\nconsole.log(Object.entries(user));\nOutput\n[\n  [\"name\", \"Amar\"],\n  [\"age\", 24]\n]\n\nInterview One-liner:\n\nObject.entries() obj ect ki s aari properti es ko [key, value] pairs ke form me return karta hai.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n \nTheory\n\nObject.hasOwn() check karta hai ki koi property object ki own property hai ya prototype se aayi hai.\n\nExample\nconst user = {\n  name: \"Amar\"\n};\n\nconsole.log(Object.hasOwn(user, \"name\"));\nconsole.log(Object.hasOwn(user, \"age\"));\nOutput\ntrue\nfalse\n\nInterview One-liner:\n\nObject.hasOwn() check karta hai ki property object ki own property hai ya nahi.",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "🟢 Object.keys()\nTheory\n\nObject.keys() object ki saari own keys (property names) ko array ke form me return karta hai.\n\nExample\nconst user = {\n  name: \"Amar\",\n  age: 24\n};\n\nconsole.log(Object.keys(user));\nOutput\n[\"name\", \"age\"]\n\nInterview One-liner:\n\nObject.keys() object ki saari own keys ko array ke form me return karta hai.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n🟢 Object.values()\nTheory\n\nObject.values() object ki saari own values ko array ke form me return karta hai.\n\nExample\nconst user = {\n  name: \"Amar\",\n  age: 24\n};\n\nconsole.log(Object.values(user));\nOutput\n[\"Amar\", 24]\n\nInterview One-liner:\n\nObject.values() object ki saari own values ko array ke form me return karta hai.\n\n\n\n\n\n \n🟢 Object.entries()\nTheory\n\nObject.entries() object ki har property ko [key, value] pair ke form me array ke andar return karta hai.\n\nExample\nconst user = {\n  name: \"Amar\",\n  age: 24\n};\n\nconsole.log(Object.entries(user));\nOutput\n[\n  [\"name\", \"Amar\"],\n  [\"age\", 24]\n]\n\nInterview One-liner:\n\nObject.entries() obj ect ki s aari properti es ko [key, value] pairs ke form me return karta hai.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n \nTheory\n\nObject.hasOwn() check karta hai ki koi property object ki own property hai ya prototype se aayi hai.\n\nExample\nconst user = {\n  name: \"Amar\"\n};\n\nconsole.log(Object.hasOwn(user, \"name\"));\nconsole.log(Object.hasOwn(user, \"age\"));\nOutput\ntrue\nfalse\n\nInterview One-liner:\n\nObject.hasOwn() check karta hai ki property object ki own property hai ya nahi.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
