    {
      "id": "76tXPLtg0l6fS8mhzmcKG",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 53457.980233318645,
      "y": -4366.617806161158,
      "width": 2712.2598253498763,
      "height": 2983.4878505360025,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#da77f2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bMq",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 167948325,
      "version": 478,
      "versionNonce": 872935819,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785575620415,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "THERE ARE 3 WAYS FOR DATA ENCAPSUALTION ::\n\n1. CLOSURES [OLDEST] \n\n2. PRIVATE MEMBERS \n\n3. GETTER / SETTERS\n\n\n\n\n@ Closures ::\\\n\n# Closures (Hinglish Theory)\n\n## Definition\n\n**Closure** JavaScript ka ek feature hai jisme **inner function apne outer function ke variables ko yaad (remember) rakhta hai, even after the outer function has finished executing.**\n\nYe possible hota hai **Lexical Scope** aur **Execution Context** ki wajah se.\n\n---\n\n## Execution Context ke saath relation\n\nJab koi function execute hota hai, uska ek **Execution Context** banta hai.\n\nIs context ke andar:\n\n* Variables\n* Parameters\n* Functions\n* Lexical Environment\n\nstore hote hain.\n\nNormally function khatam hone ke baad uska execution context remove ho jata hai.\n\nLekin agar koi **inner function** us context ke variables ko use kar raha ho, to JavaScript un variables ko memory mein preserve rakhta hai.\n\nIsi preserved lexical environment ko **Closure** kehte hain.\n\n---\n\n## Lexical Scoping ke saath relation\n\nJavaScript **Lexical Scoping** follow karti hai.\n\nIska matlab hai ki ek function apne:\n\n* khud ke variables,\n* parent function ke variables,\n* aur global variables\n\nko access kar sakta hai.\n\nClosure isi lexical scope ka use karta hai.\n\n---\n\n## Data Encapsulation\n\nPrivate fields (`#`) aane se pehle JavaScript mein data hide karne ke liye Closures use hote the.\n\nOuter function ke variables directly outside access nahi kar sakte.\n\nSirf inner function unhe access ya modify kar sakta hai.\n\nIsliye Closures ko old way of **Data Encapsulation** bhi bola jata hai.\n\n---\n\n## Uses of Closures\n\n* Data Encapsulation (Old Method)\n* Private Variables\n* Function Factory\n* Callbacks\n* Event Handlers\n* Memoization\n* Currying\n\n---\n\n## Interview Definition\n\n**Closure is a JavaScript feature in which an inner function remembers and can access the variables of its outer function even after the outer function has finished executing. This works because of Lexical\nScoping and the preserved Lexical Environment. Earlier, closures were commonly used for data encapsulation and creating private variables.**\n",
      "fontSize": 26.81786831942474,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "THERE ARE 3 WAYS FOR DATA ENCAPSUALTION ::\n\n1. CLOSURES [OLDEST] \n\n2. PRIVATE MEMBERS \n\n3. GETTER / SETTERS\n\n\n\n\n@ Closures ::\\\n\n# Closures (Hinglish Theory)\n\n## Definition\n\n**Closure** JavaScript ka ek feature hai jisme **inner function apne outer function ke variables ko yaad (remember) rakhta hai, even after the outer function has finished executing.**\n\nYe possible hota hai **Lexical Scope** aur **Execution Context** ki wajah se.\n\n---\n\n## Execution Context ke saath relation\n\nJab koi function execute hota hai, uska ek **Execution Context** banta hai.\n\nIs context ke andar:\n\n* Variables\n* Parameters\n* Functions\n* Lexical Environment\n\nstore hote hain.\n\nNormally function khatam hone ke baad uska execution context remove ho jata hai.\n\nLekin agar koi **inner function** us context ke variables ko use kar raha ho, to JavaScript un variables ko memory mein preserve rakhta hai.\n\nIsi preserved lexical environment ko **Closure** kehte hain.\n\n---\n\n## Lexical Scoping ke saath relation\n\nJavaScript **Lexical Scoping** follow karti hai.\n\nIska matlab hai ki ek function apne:\n\n* khud ke variables,\n* parent function ke variables,\n* aur global variables\n\nko access kar sakta hai.\n\nClosure isi lexical scope ka use karta hai.\n\n---\n\n## Data Encapsulation\n\nPrivate fields (`#`) aane se pehle JavaScript mein data hide karne ke liye Closures use hote the.\n\nOuter function ke variables directly outside access nahi kar sakte.\n\nSirf inner function unhe access ya modify kar sakta hai.\n\nIsliye Closures ko old way of **Data Encapsulation** bhi bola jata hai.\n\n---\n\n## Uses of Closures\n\n* Data Encapsulation (Old Method)\n* Private Variables\n* Function Factory\n* Callbacks\n* Event Handlers\n* Memoization\n* Currying\n\n---\n\n## Interview Definition\n\n**Closure is a JavaScript feature in which an inner function remembers and can access the variables of its outer function even after the outer function has finished executing. This works because of Lexical Scoping and the preserved Lexical Environment. Earlier, closures were commonly used for data encapsulation and creating private variables.**\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
