    {
      "id": "BZDYG-36ZTo-zB42q1Wqo",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 58403.26167341778,
      "y": -1192.3699328769467,
      "width": 3304.52978515625,
      "height": 1960,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#2f9e44",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bNp",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1041531845,
      "version": 3,
      "versionNonce": 425082955,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785605047101,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "⭐ Polymorphism (OOP)\n1. Meaning\n\nPolymorphism means \"One Interface, Many Forms.\"\n\nPoly = Many\nMorph = Forms\n\nIt means the same method or function can perform different behaviors depending on the object that calls it.\n\nSimple Example\n\nA start() method can behave differently:\n\nCar → Starts the engine.\nBike → Starts the bike.\nComputer → Boots the operating system.\n\nSame method name (start()), but different behavior.\n\n2. Why do we use Polymorphism?\n\nPolymorphism makes code flexible, reusable, and easier to maintain.\n\nBenefits\n✅ Write one interface for multiple objects.\n✅ Reduce duplicate code.\n✅ Easy to add new classes without changing existing code.\n✅ Makes applications scalable.\n✅ Supports Runtime Decision Making.\n3. Types of Polymorphism\n1. Compile-Time Polymorphism (Static Polymorphism)\n\nThe method to execute is decided during compilation.\n\nIt is achieved using:\n\nMethod Overloading\nOperator Overloading (in languages that support it)\n\nNote: JavaScript does not support true method overloading.\n\n2. Runtime Polymorphism (Dynamic Polymorphism)\n\nThe method to execute is decided while the program is running.\n\nIt is achieved using:\n\nMethod Overriding\nDynamic Dispatch\n\nThe child class provides its own implementation of a method that already exists in the parent class.\n\n⭐ Interview Definition\n\nPolymorphism is an OOP concept where the same method or interface can have different implementations, allowing different objects to respond differently to the same method call. It improves flexibility, reusability, and maintainability of code.",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "⭐ Polymorphism (OOP)\n1. Meaning\n\nPolymorphism means \"One Interface, Many Forms.\"\n\nPoly = Many\nMorph = Forms\n\nIt means the same method or function can perform different behaviors depending on the object that calls it.\n\nSimple Example\n\nA start() method can behave differently:\n\nCar → Starts the engine.\nBike → Starts the bike.\nComputer → Boots the operating system.\n\nSame method name (start()), but different behavior.\n\n2. Why do we use Polymorphism?\n\nPolymorphism makes code flexible, reusable, and easier to maintain.\n\nBenefits\n✅ Write one interface for multiple objects.\n✅ Reduce duplicate code.\n✅ Easy to add new classes without changing existing code.\n✅ Makes applications scalable.\n✅ Supports Runtime Decision Making.\n3. Types of Polymorphism\n1. Compile-Time Polymorphism (Static Polymorphism)\n\nThe method to execute is decided during compilation.\n\nIt is achieved using:\n\nMethod Overloading\nOperator Overloading (in languages that support it)\n\nNote: JavaScript does not support true method overloading.\n\n2. Runtime Polymorphism (Dynamic Polymorphism)\n\nThe method to execute is decided while the program is running.\n\nIt is achieved using:\n\nMethod Overriding\nDynamic Dispatch\n\nThe child class provides its own implementation of a method that already exists in the parent class.\n\n⭐ Interview Definition\n\nPolymorphism is an OOP concept where the same method or interface can have different implementations, allowing different objects to respond differently to the same method call. It improves flexibility, reusability, and maintainability of code.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "DPYXizSIWI4Aoj_hFyPTL",
      "type": "rectangle",
      "x": 58354.18747501283,
