    {
      "id": "4-auMUBE7qeSlXDtlRtHN",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 58376.41007022768,
      "y": 3731.7038095745515,
      "width": 1199.8834228515625,
      "height": 1120,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#2f9e44",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bNx",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1447995211,
      "version": 41,
      "versionNonce": 1714825611,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785605307635,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Agar Dynamic Dispatch na hota...\n\nMaan lo Parent aur Child dono me same method hai.\n\nAnimal\n   └── sound() → \"Animal Sound\"\n\nDog extends Animal\n   └── sound() → \"Bark\"\n\nAb runtime par jab tum likhte ho:\n\nAnimal a = new Dog();\na.sound();\n\nQuestion:\n\nCompiler ya Runtime ko kaise pata chale ki Animal wala sound() chalana hai ya Dog wala?\n\nAgar Dynamic Dispatch na hota, to compiler sirf reference dekhta.\n\nReference = Animal\n\nTo output aata:\n\nAnimal Sound ❌\n\nLekin actual object to Dog ka hai. Hume chahiye:\n\nDog Barks ✅\n\nIsi problem ko solve karne ke liye Dynamic Dispatch introduce kiya gaya.",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Agar Dynamic Dispatch na hota...\n\nMaan lo Parent aur Child dono me same method hai.\n\nAnimal\n   └── sound() → \"Animal Sound\"\n\nDog extends Animal\n   └── sound() → \"Bark\"\n\nAb runtime par jab tum likhte ho:\n\nAnimal a = new Dog();\na.sound();\n\nQuestion:\n\nCompiler ya Runtime ko kaise pata chale ki Animal wala sound() chalana hai ya Dog wala?\n\nAgar Dynamic Dispatch na hota, to compiler sirf reference dekhta.\n\nReference = Animal\n\nTo output aata:\n\nAnimal Sound ❌\n\nLekin actual object to Dog ka hai. Hume chahiye:\n\nDog Barks ✅\n\nIsi problem ko solve karne ke liye Dynamic Dispatch introduce kiya gaya.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "RKohv3VsOK6A-uivih_Rv",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 58402.017675,
      "y": 5306.182721928068,
      "width": 2325.0625,
      "height": 3290,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#f08c00",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bNy",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 47935173,
      "version": 6,
      "versionNonce": 455381925,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785605364452,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "What is Dynamic Dispatch?\n\nDynamic Dispatch is the runtime process of deciding which overridden method should be executed. The decision is made based on the actual object, not on the reference.\n\nSimple words me:\n\nMethod call same hoti hai, lekin runtime par object dekhkar decide hota hai ki Parent ka method chalega ya Child ka. Is process ko Dynamic Dispatch kehte hain.\n\nIsliye isko Dynamic (runtime me decide hota hai) aur Dispatch (correct method ko call karna) kaha jata hai.\n\n⭐ Parent Reference → Child Object Concept\n\nYe concept statically typed languages (Java, C#) me bahut common hai.\n\nExample:\n\nAnimal animal = new Dog();\n\nYahan:\n\nAnimal → Parent Reference\nnew Dog() → Child Object\n\nAb agar hum likhen:\n\nanimal.sound();\n\nTo output hoga:\n\nDog Barks\n\nQuestion: Reference to Animal ka tha, fir Dog ka method kyu chala?\n\nAnswer: Kyunki runtime par JVM ne dekha ki actual object Dog ka hai, isliye Dog ka overridden sound() method execute kiya.\n\nYahi Dynamic Method Dispatch hai.\n\n⭐ Runtime Method Selection\n\nDynamic Dispatch me compiler pehle se method fix nahi karta.\n\nJab program run hota hai, tab ye steps follow hote hain:\n\nStep 1\n\nMethod call hoti hai.\n\nanimal.sound();\n\n↓\n\nStep 2\n\nRuntime check karta hai:\n\nActual object kis class ka hai?\n\n↓\n\nStep 3\n\nAgar Child class ne method override kiya hai,\n\n↓\n\nStep 4\n\nTo Child ka method execute hoga.\n\nAgar override nahi kiya hai,\n\n↓\n\nStep 5\n\nTo Parent ka original method execute hoga.\n\n⭐ Flow Diagram\nMethod Call\n     │\n     ▼\nRuntime checks actual object\n     │\n     ▼\nIs method overridden?\n     │\n ┌───┴────┐\n │        │\nYes      No\n │        │\n ▼        ▼\nChild    Parent\nMethod   Method\nExecutes Executes",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "What is Dynamic Dispatch?\n\nDynamic Dispatch is the runtime process of deciding which overridden method should be executed. The decision is made based on the actual object, not on the reference.\n\nSimple words me:\n\nMethod call same hoti hai, lekin runtime par object dekhkar decide hota hai ki Parent ka method chalega ya Child ka. Is process ko Dynamic Dispatch kehte hain.\n\nIsliye isko Dynamic (runtime me decide hota hai) aur Dispatch (correct method ko call karna) kaha jata hai.\n\n⭐ Parent Reference → Child Object Concept\n\nYe concept statically typed languages (Java, C#) me bahut common hai.\n\nExample:\n\nAnimal animal = new Dog();\n\nYahan:\n\nAnimal → Parent Reference\nnew Dog() → Child Object\n\nAb agar hum likhen:\n\nanimal.sound();\n\nTo output hoga:\n\nDog Barks\n\nQuestion: Reference to Animal ka tha, fir Dog ka method kyu chala?\n\nAnswer: Kyunki runtime par JVM ne dekha ki actual object Dog ka hai, isliye Dog ka overridden sound() method execute kiya.\n\nYahi Dynamic Method Dispatch hai.\n\n⭐ Runtime Method Selection\n\nDynamic Dispatch me compiler pehle se method fix nahi karta.\n\nJab program run hota hai, tab ye steps follow hote hain:\n\nStep 1\n\nMethod call hoti hai.\n\nanimal.sound();\n\n↓\n\nStep 2\n\nRuntime check karta hai:\n\nActual object kis class ka hai?\n\n↓\n\nStep 3\n\nAgar Child class ne method override kiya hai,\n\n↓\n\nStep 4\n\nTo Child ka method execute hoga.\n\nAgar override nahi kiya hai,\n\n↓\n\nStep 5\n\nTo Parent ka original method execute hoga.\n\n⭐ Flow Diagram\nMethod Call\n     │\n     ▼\nRuntime checks actual object\n     │\n     ▼\nIs method overridden?\n     │\n ┌───┴────┐\n │        │\nYes      No\n │        │\n ▼        ▼\nChild    Parent\nMethod   Method\nExecutes Executes",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
