    {
      "id": "XRDRQexUNwGmRPQZXCOI1",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 63216.66096466703,
      "y": -7045.965566024906,
      "width": 2132.590576171875,
      "height": 2625,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#2f9e44",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bO8",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 429815781,
      "version": 5,
      "versionNonce": 761735371,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1785660969581,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "# Abstraction\n\n**Abstraction** OOPs ka ek principle hai jo mainly **\"What is doing\"** par focus karta hai, **\"How it is doing\"** par nahi.\n\nIska main purpose class ki **internal implementation (mechanism)** ko hide karna hota hai aur sirf required functionality ko expose karna hota hai.\n\nSimple words mein:\n\n> **User ko sirf yeh pata hota hai ki object kya kaam karega, lekin uske andar woh kaam kaise ho raha hai yeh nahi pata hota.**\n\nAbstraction ko achieve karne ke liye mainly **Abstract Class** aur **Interface** ka use kiya jata hai.\n\n---\n\n# Why do we use Abstraction?\n\n1. **Code Reusability** - Common code ko baar-baar likhne ki zarurat nahi padti.\n2. **Security** - Internal implementation ko hide karta hai.\n3. **Hide Internal Structure** - User ko sirf required functionality dikhai deti hai.\n4. **Maintainability** - Code ko maintain aur update karna easy ho jata hai.\n5. **Better Organization** - Code clean aur properly organized rehta hai.\n\n---\n\n# Abstract Class\n\n**Abstract Class** ek **blueprint (base class)** hoti hai jo dusri classes ke liye common structure provide karti hai.\n\nIsme hum common properties, constructors aur methods likh sakte hain jo sab child classes reuse karti hain.\n\nAbstract Class ke andar hum:\n\n* Variables\n* Constructors\n* Normal Methods\n* Abstract Methods\n\nsab rakh sakte hain.\n\nAbstract Class ka main purpose hai:\n\n* Common code ko ek jagah rakhna.\n* Abstract methods ke through child classes par rules enforce karna.\n\n**Abstract Class ka direct object create nahi kar sakte**, kyunki yeh ek incomplete class hoti hai.\n\n---\n\n# Abstract Method\n\n**Abstract Method** woh method hota hai jiska **sirf declaration hota hai, implementation (body) nahi hoti.**\n\nYeh child classes ke liye **rule** ki tarah kaam karta hai.\n\nAgar koi child class abstract class ko inherit karti hai, to usko **saare abstract methods implement karne hi padte hain**, warna compiler error de deta hai.\n\nIsse consistency maintain rehti hai aur har child class required functionality zaroor provide karti hai.\n\n---\n\n# Interface\n\n**Interface** ek **pure contract (set of rules)** hota hai.\n\nYeh sirf batata hai ki **kaun-kaun se methods hone chahiye**, lekin un methods ka implementation kaise hoga, yeh nahi batata.\n\nInterface ke andar generally sirf rules define kiye jaate hain.\n\nJo class interface ko implement karti hai, usko interface ke saare methods ka implementation dena compulsory hota hai.\n\nSimple words mein:\n\n> **Abstract Class = Common Code + Rules**\n> **Interface = Sirf Rules (Contract)**\n",
      "fontSize": 28,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "# Abstraction\n\n**Abstraction** OOPs ka ek principle hai jo mainly **\"What is doing\"** par focus karta hai, **\"How it is doing\"** par nahi.\n\nIska main purpose class ki **internal implementation (mechanism)** ko hide karna hota hai aur sirf required functionality ko expose karna hota hai.\n\nSimple words mein:\n\n> **User ko sirf yeh pata hota hai ki object kya kaam karega, lekin uske andar woh kaam kaise ho raha hai yeh nahi pata hota.**\n\nAbstraction ko achieve karne ke liye mainly **Abstract Class** aur **Interface** ka use kiya jata hai.\n\n---\n\n# Why do we use Abstraction?\n\n1. **Code Reusability** - Common code ko baar-baar likhne ki zarurat nahi padti.\n2. **Security** - Internal implementation ko hide karta hai.\n3. **Hide Internal Structure** - User ko sirf required functionality dikhai deti hai.\n4. **Maintainability** - Code ko maintain aur update karna easy ho jata hai.\n5. **Better Organization** - Code clean aur properly organized rehta hai.\n\n---\n\n# Abstract Class\n\n**Abstract Class** ek **blueprint (base class)** hoti hai jo dusri classes ke liye common structure provide karti hai.\n\nIsme hum common properties, constructors aur methods likh sakte hain jo sab child classes reuse karti hain.\n\nAbstract Class ke andar hum:\n\n* Variables\n* Constructors\n* Normal Methods\n* Abstract Methods\n\nsab rakh sakte hain.\n\nAbstract Class ka main purpose hai:\n\n* Common code ko ek jagah rakhna.\n* Abstract methods ke through child classes par rules enforce karna.\n\n**Abstract Class ka direct object create nahi kar sakte**, kyunki yeh ek incomplete class hoti hai.\n\n---\n\n# Abstract Method\n\n**Abstract Method** woh method hota hai jiska **sirf declaration hota hai, implementation (body) nahi hoti.**\n\nYeh child classes ke liye **rule** ki tarah kaam karta hai.\n\nAgar koi child class abstract class ko inherit karti hai, to usko **saare abstract methods implement karne hi padte hain**, warna compiler error de deta hai.\n\nIsse consistency maintain rehti hai aur har child class required functionality zaroor provide karti hai.\n\n---\n\n# Interface\n\n**Interface** ek **pure contract (set of rules)** hota hai.\n\nYeh sirf batata hai ki **kaun-kaun se methods hone chahiye**, lekin un methods ka implementation kaise hoga, yeh nahi batata.\n\nInterface ke andar generally sirf rules define kiye jaate hain.\n\nJo class interface ko implement karti hai, usko interface ke saare methods ka implementation dena compulsory hota hai.\n\nSimple words mein:\n\n> **Abstract Class = Common Code + Rules**\n> **Interface = Sirf Rules (Contract)**\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
